Deploying IIS Web Server on Azure Windows VM and Creating a Reusable Image
________________________________________
Document Control
•	Title: Deploying IIS on Windows VM & Creating Image in Azure
•	Version: 1.0
•	Author: Aditya Yadav
•	Date: 18-Aug-2025
________________________________________
Objective
The objective of this SOP is to provision a Windows Virtual Machine (VM) on Microsoft Azure, configure it with IIS (Internet Information Services) web server, and create a reusable custom image from this VM to ensure consistent deployments.
________________________________________
Scope
This SOP is intended for Cloud/DevOps Engineers working on Azure Virtual Machines with Windows OS. It provides a step-by-step guide for:
•	Provisioning a Windows VM in Azure
•	Installing IIS Web Server
•	Verifying web server accessibility
•	Creating a VM Image (equivalent to AWS AMI)
•	Deploying another VM from the captured image
________________________________________
Prerequisites
•	Active Azure Subscription
•	Access to Azure Portal
•	Permissions to create VM, Storage, and Networking resources
•	Remote Desktop (RDP) client installed on local system
________________________________________
Procedure
Step 1: Create a Windows Virtual Machine
1.	Log in to the Azure Portal (https://portal.azure.com).
2.	Navigate to Virtual Machines → Create → Azure Virtual Machine.
3.	Select the following:
o	Resource Group: Create new or use existing
o	Image: Windows Server 2019/2022 Data-Centre
o	Size: Standard B1s (for testing)
o	Username/Password: Set admin credentials
o	Inbound Ports: Allow 3389 (RDP) and 80 (HTTP)
4.	Click Review + Create → Create.
5.	Once deployment completes, go to the VM Overview page.
________________________________________
Step 2: Connect to VM
1.	In Azure Portal, go to the VM → Click Connect → RDP.
2.	Download the RDP file and connect using the credentials created.
________________________________________
Step 3: Install IIS Web Server
1.	Inside Windows VM → Open Server Manager.
2.	Click Add Roles and Features → Select Web Server (IIS).
3.	Complete the installation.
4.	Verify by opening Internet Explorer/Edge inside the VM and browsing:
o	http://localhost
o	You should see the IIS Default Welcome Page.
________________________________________
Step 4: Verify IIS from Browser
1.	Copy the Public IP Address of the VM from Azure Portal.
2.	Paste into your local system browser:
o	http:// 4.240.94.79  <Public_IP>
o	The IIS Default Page should load successfully.
 
________________________________________
Step 5: Capture Image of VM

1.	Stop the running VM (required for image capture).
1.	In Azure Portal, stop the VM (deallocate).
2.	Navigate to the VM → Click Capture.
3.	Fill details:
o	Shared Image Gallery: Select/Create → SIG-IIS.
o	Image Definition Name: WinIIS-Image.
o	Publisher: YourCompany
o	Offer: WinIIS
o	SKU: IIS-Web
o	OS State: Generalized.
o	Select Automatically delete this VM after capture (if not needed).
	Image Versioning (Important Section)
When creating the image version, fill the details as:

o	Version number: 1.0.0
o	Exclude from latest: ❌ (Unchecked)
o	End of life date: (leave blank for now)
o	Block deletion before End of life: (ignore, not applicable)
o	Lock deleting Replicated Locations: ❌ (Unchecked)
o	Shallow replication: ❌ (Unchecked → use default deep replication)
 

This ensures the captured image is available as the latest version for new VM deployments.

	Version Control
•	First image: 1.0.0
•	If IIS configuration changes: increment to 1.0.1.
•	If new software added: increment to 1.1.0.
•	Major rebuild: increment to 2.0.0.

	Note
•	Always deallocate VM before capturing image.
•	Do not set End of Life unless you know when this image should expire.
•	Avoid using Shallow Replication unless testing.
________________________________________

________________________________________
Step 6: Deploy Another VM from Image
1.	Navigate to Azure Portal → Images → Select Win-IIS-Base-Image.
2.	Click + Create VM.
3.	Follow the wizard → Configure username, password, and network.
4.	Deploy the VM.

	Deploy New VM from Custom Image
1.	Navigate to Shared Image Gallery → Select WinIIS-Image.
2.	Choose + Create VM.
3.	Configure:
o	VM Name: WinVM-IIS-FromImage.
o	Size, Authentication, Networking as required.
4.	Deploy → Start VM.
5.	Connect via RDP → Open browser → http://localhost or use Public IP.
6.	Verify IIS is running (your custom webpage should display).

________________________________________
Step 7: Validate IIS on New VM
1.	Once deployed, copy the Public IP of the new VM.
2.	Open it in browser: http://20.83.163.186<New_VM_Public_IP>. 
3.	IIS Default Page should be displayed, confirming IIS is pre-configured.

 
	Validation
•	New VM deployed from the image should have IIS pre-installed.
•	IIS default page or custom HTML should load successfully.

________________________________________
Expected Outcome
•	A Windows VM with IIS Web Server is deployed successfully.
•	A reusable Azure Image (equivalent to AWS AMI) is created.
•	Any VM launched from this image has IIS pre-installed and ready to use.
________________________________________
Rollback/Recovery Plan
•	If IIS fails to load, check:
o	NSG inbound rules (allow port 80).
o	IIS service status inside VM (services. MSc).
•	If VM deployment fails, verify quota/region availability.
